  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry and very warm through Wednesday.\n\nWeak high pressure south of the region today slides offshore as mid-\nlevel ridging builds over the Deep South trough Wednesday. This is \ngoing to allow a shortwave troughs to quickly slide just north of \nthe area Wednesday afternoon into Wednesday evening. Well above \nnormal temperatures today and Wednesday as highs reach up to the \n80s. Dry conditions through Wednesday afternoon, then could see a \nfew showers in the Alleghenies as a front approaches from the north.\n\nA large surface high moves over southern Ontario on Thursday, which \nwill wedge that front south and stall over our area. Cloudy and \npassing showers are likely Thursday as temps return to near normal \nvalues in the 60s. This will be a short-lived and weak CAD event as \nthe high moves offshore Thursday night and southerly winds\nreturn.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A strong front moves through the region late \nweek, bringing strong winds and cold conditions.\n\nA low pressure system is forecast to track from the Great Lakes \nregion into Canada Thursday night into Friday, pushing a cold front \nacross the forecast area on Friday. This frontal system brings \nincreased precipitation chances across the forecast area Thursday \nand Friday, with a few rumbles of thunder possible as the cold front \npushes through on Friday. Additionally, high temperatures in the 60s \nto low 80s cool to the 40s and 50s on Saturday as noticeably colder \nair moves in. Behind the front, temperatures plummet into the 30s \nfor most Saturday night with only the metro areas staying in the low \n40s. Temperatures will gradually warm on Sunday with highs in the \n50s to low 60s and overnight lows in the upper 30s to 40s.  \n\nBehind the frontal system, surface high pressure builds over the \ncentral CONUS beginning on Friday before shifting eastward. A tight \npressure gradient between the surface high and the departing low \npressure system leads to windy conditions across the forecast area \nFriday night into Saturday. Northwest winds gust 20 to 30 miles per \nhour , with higher elevations gusting up to 40 mph. Wind headlines \nmay be needed across the higher elevations but we will continue to \nmonitor. \n\nIt's also worth mentioning that with gusty northwest winds, upslope \nsnow showers are possible along the Alleghenies late Friday night \ninto Saturday morning. While snow showers are unlikely, a rain/snow \nmix cannot be ruled out.  Little to no accumulation is expected \nalthough the first flakes of the season are possible.",
